\subsection*{Part A-1. Starch serial dilution}
 \begin{steps}
        \newstep[] In the following you will prepare a series of starch dilutions with concentrations: 1\%, 0.8\%, 0.6\%, 0.5\%, and 0.25\%.
        \newstep[] Find five jumbo test tubes with stoppers and label them as A, B, C, D, and E.
        \newstep[] Using a 10mL Mohr's pipette, pipet 10ml of distilled water into test tubes B, C, D, and E.

       \newstep[] Obtain about 25 mL of 2\% starch in a small beaker. Clean and rinse a 10mL Mohr's pipette with the enzyme solution.
       
         \import{\chapterlabel/}{goodlabpractice}
       
     \newstep[] Using the Mohr's pipet, pipet 10ml of starch into test tube A (test tube A is the starch 1\% solution) and 10ml into test tube B. 
      \newstep[] Pipet 10ml of test tube B into test tube C. Close test tube C with a stopper, and mix well its content by inverting the tube 3 times at constant speed. Test tube C is the starch 0.5\% solution. 
      \newstep[] Pipet 10ml of test tube C into test tube D. Close test tube D with a stopper, and mix well its content by inverting the tube 3 times at constant speed. Test tube D is the starch 0.25\% solution. 
      \newstep[] Pipet 10ml of test tube D into test tube E. Close test tube E with a stopper, and mix well its content by inverting the tube 3 times at constant speed. Test tube E is the starch 0.125\% solution. Now discart 10ml of test tube E.
      
      \newstep[] Add 10 drop of the \ce{I_2}-\ce{I^-} solution into each test and mix all test tubes well until the color is homogeneous. With your serial dilution done properly, all test tubes should have 10mL of liquid and you should see test tube A darker in comparison to the other test tubes with intensity decreasing from test tube A to E.
      
\end{steps}
